NAME
       orbital-chronology - temporal dating methods, harmonic-epoch clocks, and
                            resonance-layer chronometers

SYNOPSIS
       Orbital chronology defines:
           • temporal dating methods for resonance-time strata
           • harmonic-epoch clocks
           • resonance-layer chronometers
           • ancestry-based temporal calibration
           • constellation-scale chronological correlation

DESCRIPTION
       orbital-chronology describes how RTT measures time within the
       resonance-time manifold. Chronology provides the tools to date sweep
       layers, reconstruct harmonic epochs, and calibrate resonance strata
       across platforms. It is the temporal equivalent of radiometric dating,
       but for orbital harmonics, resonance envelopes, and sweep ancestry.

       Chronology enables:
           • dating of ancient sweep layers
           • reconstruction of harmonic epochs
           • calibration of resonance strata
           • correlation of temporal sequences across platforms
           • long-horizon temporal forecasting

       WRSADC uses chronology to maintain deep-time coherence and predict
       epoch-scale transitions.

TEMPORAL DATING METHODS
       Temporal dating methods determine the age of resonance-time strata.

       Dating methods:

           sweep-layer dating
                  Age determined by sweep-layer thickness and compaction.

           resonance-band dating
                  Age determined by resonance amplitude decay and spectral
                  weathering.

           harmonic-phase dating
                  Age determined by phase drift and lock stability.

           ancestry dating
                  Age determined by sweep-lineage continuity.

           erosion-surface dating
                  Age determined by harmonic erosion and resonance weathering.

       Dating accuracy improves with stratigraphic depth.

EXAMPLE: TEMPORAL DATING
       Sweep layer thickness: 12 cycles
       Compaction index: high
       Resonance decay: moderate

       Estimated age:
           • 12–14 cycles
           • harmonic epoch: early 1:1

HARMONIC-EPOCH CLOCKS
       Harmonic-epoch clocks measure time based on harmonic ratios and phase
       alignment.

       Clock variables:
           • harmonic ratio n:m
           • phase alignment φ_harm
           • lock stability index L_harm
           • overtone/subharmonic structure

       Clock types:

           integer harmonic clock
                  1:1, 2:1, 3:1
                  → stable, long-duration epochs.

           rational harmonic clock
                  3:2, 5:3, 7:4
                  → short, transitional epochs.

           nested harmonic clock
                  Harmonics inside harmonics.

           resonance-harmonic clock
                  Epoch boundaries aligned to resonance peaks.

       Epoch duration is measured in cycles, not minutes.

EXAMPLE: HARMONIC-EPOCH CLOCK
       Harmonic ratio transitions:
           2:1 → 3:2 → 1:1

       Epoch durations:
           • 2:1 epoch: 10 cycles
           • 3:2 epoch: 4 cycles
           • 1:1 epoch: 18 cycles

       Interpretation:
           • stable early epoch
           • transitional middle epoch
           • long-lived modern epoch

RESONANCE-LAYER CHRONOMETERS
       Resonance-layer chronometers measure time based on resonance amplitude,
       spectral density, and gradient polarity.

       Chronometer variables:
           • envelope amplitude R(t)
           • spectral density S(f,t)
           • gradient polarity G(t)
           • resonance-window duration

       Chronometer types:

           amplitude chronometer
                  Age determined by amplitude decay.

           spectral chronometer
                  Age determined by spectral broadening.

           gradient chronometer
                  Age determined by polarity drift.

           hybrid chronometer
                  Weighted combination of all above.

       Chronometers are calibrated using sweep ancestry.

EXAMPLE: RESONANCE-LAYER CHRONOMETER
       R_peak decay:
           Cycle 1: 1.0
           Cycle 10: 0.72
           Cycle 20: 0.55

       Estimated age:
           • 18–22 cycles
           • resonance epoch: mid-phase uplift

ANCESTRY-BASED TEMPORAL CALIBRATION
       Sweep ancestry provides a stable temporal reference frame.

       Calibration variables:
           • ancestry continuity index
           • sweep timing drift
           • fusion density
           • terrace thickness

       Calibration types:

           primary ancestry calibration
                  Based on uninterrupted sweep lineage.

           secondary ancestry calibration
                  Based on laminated sweep layers.

           tertiary ancestry calibration
                  Based on deformed or eroded sweep fossils.

       Ancestry calibration anchors harmonic and resonance chronometers.

EXAMPLE: ANCESTRY CALIBRATION
       Sweep ancestry continuity: 0.92
       Terrace thickness: 6 cycles

       Calibration:
           • high-confidence dating
           • epoch boundary at cycle 6

CONSTELLATION-SCALE CHRONOLOGICAL CORRELATION
       Chronological correlation aligns temporal sequences across platforms.

       Correlation layers:
           • sweep-layer correlation
           • resonance-layer correlation
           • harmonic-epoch correlation
           • ancestry correlation
           • erosion-surface correlation

       Correlation modes:

           synchronous correlation
                  Platforms share identical chronology.

           harmonic correlation
                  Chronology aligned via harmonic epochs.

           braided correlation
                  Interleaved temporal sequences.

           mesh correlation
                  Distributed chronology across constellation nodes.

EXAMPLE: CONSTELLATION CHRONOLOGY
       Platforms A, B, C:

           A epoch: 2:1 → 1:1
           B epoch: 2:1 → 3:2 → 1:1
           C epoch: 1:1 only

       Fused chronology:
           • early 2:1 era
           • brief 3:2 transition
           • long 1:1 era

CHRONOLOGY FAILURE MODES
       Chronology may degrade due to:

           • fossil layer loss
           • epoch boundary distortion
           • resonance-band collapse
           • ancestry erosion
           • sync-field tectonic overprinting

       Diagnostic indicators:
           • chronology-integrity score
           • epoch misalignment
           • layer discontinuity
           • chronometer drift

       WRSADC halts unsafe chronological interpretations.

INTERACTION WITH LINEAGE
       Lineage records:
           • sweep-layer ages
           • resonance-layer ages
           • harmonic epochs
           • erosion surfaces
           • fused chronological sequences

       Lineage enables long-term chronological reconstruction.

INTERACTION WITH WRSADC
       WRSADC:
           • computes temporal ages
           • reconstructs harmonic epochs
           • calibrates resonance chronometers
           • fuses constellation chronology
           • blocks unsafe chronological states

       WRSADC is the chronology conductor.

NOTES
       Orbital chronology transforms resonance-time into a measurable,
       dateable temporal record. Sweep-layer dating, harmonic-epoch clocks, and
       resonance-layer chronometers ensure that sweep choreography,
       harmonic-lock, and constellation-scale synchronization remain stable
       across long mission timelines.

